Okay, let's start with step 1. Dr. Banks has 330 toothbrushes and gives away 53 in January, which leaves him with 330 - 53 = 277 toothbrushes. Then he gives away 67 in February, so that's 277 - 67 = 210 remaining. In March, he gives away another 46, bringing it down to 210 - 46 = 164 toothbrushes left. The next month is April and May, where he gives away the remaining toothbrushes, half each month. Half of 164 is 82 in April and 82 in May. So, comparing the months, January saw him giving away 53, February 67, March 46, April and May each had 82. The busiest month was February with 67, and the slowest was January with 53. The difference is 67 - 53 = 14 toothbrushes more in February than in January. So, Dr. Banks gave out 14 more toothbrushes in February than in January.
